    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:candidate-v7-npl-1220-armed-court","source_ref":"source:npl-1220-1229","anchor":{"section":"6728 [1220]","record_id":"vsevolod-court-armour"},"note":"The chronicle says that Prince Vsevolod came from Gorodishche with his whole court, armed in armour, to Yaroslav’s court. This is one named 1220 conflict context, not a weapon inventory, protection value, military role, skill, current troop or battle result.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:candidate-v7-npl-1220-five-regiments","source_ref":"source:npl-1220-1229","anchor":{"section":"6728 [1220]","record_id":"tverdislav-five-regiments"},"note":"The chronicle says Tverdislav’s supporters stood around him in five regiments. It records one crisis arrangement, not a standing Novgorod military organization, mobilization rule, force size or current unit.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:candidate-v7-npl-1220-mediation","source_ref":"source:npl-1220-1229","anchor":{"section":"6728 [1220]","record_id":"mitrofan-mediation"},"note":"The chronicle says Archbishop Mitrofan brought the sides to peace and that the prince and Tverdislav kissed the cross. It is one narrated reconciliation, not a general ecclesiastical competence or procedure.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:candidate-v7-npl-1220-posadnik-succession","source_ref":"source:npl-1220-1229","anchor":{"section":"6728 [1220]","record_id":"tverdislav-ivanko-posadnichestvo"},"note":"The chronicle says Tverdislav relinquished the posadnichestvo and Ivanko Dmitrovich received it. It does not establish election, tenure, jurisdiction, hierarchy or a current office-holder.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:candidate-v7-npl-1222-delegation","source_ref":"source:npl-1220-1229","anchor":{"section":"6730 [1222]","record_id":"mitrofan-ivanko-elders-vladimir"},"note":"The chronicle records that Novgorod sent Archbishop Mitrofan, Posadnik Ivanko and senior men to Yuri Vsevolodovich in Vladimir. It is a named delegation, not a general diplomatic institution or authority.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:candidate-v7-npl-1222-princely-grant","source_ref":"source:npl-1220-1229","anchor":{"section":"6730 [1222]","record_id":"yuri-gives-vsevolod"},"note":"The chronicle says Yuri gave his son Vsevolod ‘on all the will of Novgorod’. This is source wording for one 1222 succession event, not a constitutional rule, office, right or present ruler.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:candidate-v7-npl-1222-kesi-campaign","source_ref":"source:npl-1220-1229","anchor":{"section":"6730 [1222]","record_id":"novgorod-svyatoslav-kesi"},"note":"The chronicle records Novgorodians with Svyatoslav going to Kesi, Lithuanians coming to help, and the town not being taken. It does not establish a standing alliance, current route, troop, siege capability or result elsewhere.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:candidate-v7-npl-1223-colyvan-campaign","source_ref":"source:npl-1220-1229","anchor":{"section":"6731 [1223]","record_id":"yaroslav-colyvan"},"note":"The chronicle says Prince Yaroslav came to Kolivan with his whole oblast, ravaged the Chud land and did not take the town. This is one named campaign, not a general military policy or current expedition.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:candidate-v7-npl-1223-colyvan-aftermath","source_ref":"source:npl-1220-1229","anchor":{"section":"6731 [1223]","record_id":"colyvan-captives-gold"},"note":"In the same 1223 account the chronicle says captives and much gold were brought back. This is a narrated aftermath of that campaign, not a lawful entitlement, ordinary outcome or present booty.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:candidate-v7-npl-1220-wife-children","source_ref":"source:npl-1220-1229","anchor":{"section":"6728 [1220]","record_id":"tverdislav-wife-children-monastic"},"note":"The chronicle says Tverdislav secretly left wife and children for a monastery, and then says his wife entered another monastery. This records one household event, not a marital, gender, parental or monastic norm.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:candidate-v7-gramota9-property","source_ref":"source:gramota9","anchor":{"record_id":"novgorod/9","section":"Whole private letter"},"note":"The letter reports Gostyata saying that her father and kin gave her property. It is an asserted fact inside one letter, not a property, inheritance, gender or marriage rule.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:candidate-v7-gramota9-another-wife","source_ref":"source:gramota9","anchor":{"record_id":"novgorod/9","section":"Whole private letter"},"note":"The letter reports Gostyata being driven away after another wife was taken. It is one complaint’s content, not evidence of marriage formation, divorce, polygyny, sanction or a present relationship.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:candidate-v7-gramota531-anna-kin-request","source_ref":"source:gramota531","anchor":{"record_id":"novgorod/531","section":"Catalogue metadata"},"note":"Catalogue metadata identifies an early-thirteenth-century private letter from Anna to her brother Klimyata requesting protection against an alleged accusation. This is one named kin-directed request, not a status, legal or household system.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:candidate-v7-gramota778-alphabet","source_ref":"source:gramota778","anchor":{"record_id":"novgorod/778","section":"Catalogue metadata"},"note":"Catalogue metadata classifies one early-thirteenth-century Novgorod record as an alphabet exercise. It does not establish school, curriculum, literacy rate, a learner or a text in a scene.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:candidate-v7-gramota907-theft-inquiry","source_ref":"source:gramota907","anchor":{"record_id":"novgorod/907","section":"Catalogue metadata"},"note":"Catalogue metadata identifies an earlier letter to a posadnik about a theft inquiry. It is not a procedural code, jurisdiction, sanction or current accusation.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:candidate-v7-museum-archaeology-collection","source_ref":"source:candidate-v7-novgorod-museum-city-underfoot","anchor":{"section":"О выставке","record_id":"x-xvii-archaeology-collection"},"note":"The museum describes about 100 objects dated X–XVII centuries from archaeological collections and excavations in Veliky Novgorod, including toys, writing tablets and styli. This broad collection context does not put any object in 1230 or in a scene.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:candidate-v7-museum-onfim-learning","source_ref":"source:candidate-v7-novgorod-museum-city-underfoot","anchor":{"section":"О выставке","record_id":"onfim-writing-reading-counting"},"note":"The museum’s exhibition text describes Onfim’s birch-bark letters as ones in which he learned writing, reading and counting. It is museum exposition context, not a dated school, curriculum, literacy rate or present child.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:candidate-v7-museum-individual-toys","source_ref":"source:candidate-v7-novgorod-museum-city-underfoot","anchor":{"section":"О выставке","record_id":"individual-toys"},"note":"The museum describes individual toys represented by weapon and transport imitations and lists figurative, sound and mechanical toys. This is archaeological display context, not an actual weapon, toy, child, craft skill or scene activity.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:candidate-v7-museum-collective-games","source_ref":"source:candidate-v7-novgorod-museum-city-underfoot","anchor":{"section":"О выставке","record_id":"leather-balls-wooden-spinning-tops"},"note":"The museum identifies leather balls and wooden spinning tops as toys for collective games. It does not establish a game’s rule, player, prevalence, availability or current play.","review_status":"approved"},
